## `n_before_user` — Not Present in BeeLlama

The `n_before_user` field referenced in the working record (#107) **does not exist** in the beellama fork. A search across the entire repository turns up zero hits. This field was either removed or never existed in the beellama codebase — it may have been a concept discussed in upstream that was never merged into the version beellama tracks.

## Checkpoint Behavior in BeeLlama

BeeLlama's checkpoint system has three layers, all present and functional:

### 1. In-Memory Context Checkpoints (`common_prompt_checkpoint`)

These are KV cache snapshots stored per-slot in memory. The key struct is in `common/common.h:1214`:

```cpp
struct common_prompt_checkpoint {
    int64_t n_tokens;
    int id_task = -1;
    llama_pos pos_min;
    llama_pos pos_max;

    std::vector<uint8_t> data_tgt;   // target model KV cache bytes
    std::vector<uint8_t> data_dft;   // draft model KV cache bytes
    std::vector<uint8_t> data_spec;  // speculative-decoding state (e.g. eagle3)
    // ...
};
```

Each slot maintains a list of these: `server-task.h:617`:

```cpp
struct server_prompt {
    server_tokens tokens;
    std::list<common_prompt_checkpoint> checkpoints;
    // ...
};
```

### 2. Checkpoint Creation Logic (`server-context.cpp:2516`)

Checkpoints are created during prompt evaluation. The creation is gated by several conditions (from `server-context.cpp:3880-3912`):

```cpp
// skip ordinary mid-prompt checkpoints, unless the batch starts a user
// message or we are near the end of the prompt
if (!is_user_start && !near_prompt_end) {
    do_checkpoint = false;
}

// no need to create checkpoints that are too close together, unless it's the last user message
do_checkpoint = do_checkpoint && (
        slot.prompt.checkpoints.empty() ||
        is_last_user_message || near_prompt_end ||
        n_tokens_start > slot.prompt.checkpoints.back().n_tokens + params_base.checkpoint_min_step);
```

Key parameters from `common/common.h:736-737`:
```cpp
int32_t n_ctx_checkpoints   = 32;    // max number of context checkpoints per slot
int32_t checkpoint_min_step = 8192;  // minimum spacing between context checkpoints
```

### 3. Checkpoint Restoration During Context Shift (`server-context.cpp:3585-3620`)

When the context window is full and tokens need to be shifted, the system searches for a viable checkpoint to restore from rather than starting from scratch:

```cpp
// search for a context checkpoint
const auto it = std::find_if(
    slot.prompt.checkpoints.rbegin(),
    slot.prompt.checkpoints.rend(),
    [&](const auto & cur) {
        // guarantee that a checkpoint will result in at least one token being processed [TAG_PROMPT_LOGITS]
        SLT_TRC(slot, "checking checkpoint with [%d, %d] against %d...\n", cur.pos_min, cur.pos_max, pos_min_thold);
        // workaround for [TAG_CHECKPOINTS_FIX_POS_MIN]
        if (cur.pos_max > pos_next) {
            return false;
        }
        return cur.pos_min < pos_min_thold || cur.pos_min == 0;
    }
);
```

The `pos_min_thold` is computed relative to sliding window attention:

```cpp
// from earlier in the function (~line 2990)
// pos_min_thold = pos_next - n_swa
```

### 4. Checkpoint Invalidation

After a context shift, any checkpoints whose `pos_max` exceeds the new `pos_next` are erased (`server-context.cpp:3624-3631`):

```cpp
// erase any checkpoints with pos_max > pos_next
for (auto it = slot.prompt.checkpoints.begin(); it != slot.prompt.checkpoints.end();) {
    const auto & cur = *it;
    if (cur.pos_max > pos_next) {
        SLT_TRC(slot, "erased invalidated context checkpoint (pos_min = %d, pos_max = %d, n_tokens = %" PRId64 ", n_swa = %d, pos_next = %d, size = %.3f MiB)\n",
                cur.pos_min, cur.pos_max, cur.n_tokens, n_swa, pos_next, (float) cur.size() / 1024 / 1024);
        it = slot.prompt.checkpoints.erase(it);
    } else {
        ++it;
    }
}
```

### 5. Known SWA Bug

There's an acknowledged TODO (`[TAG_CHECKPOINTS_FIX_POS_MIN]`) that checkpoint `pos_min` is incorrectly determined for Sliding Window Attention models — the saved data doesn't actually cover the `[pos_min, pos_max]` range as claimed. This is tracked upstream at `llama.cpp/pull/24411`.

### 6. Disk-Based Prompt Cache (Separate Mechanism)

Beyond in-memory checkpoints, there's a second layer: the on-disk prompt cache (`server_prompt_cache`) which saves slot state to disk using `llama_state_seq_save_file`. This is triggered when slots are evicted (LRU or similarity-based) and is controlled by `cache_ram_mib` and the `f_keep > 0.5` threshold.

