## How the Server's Prompt Cache / Slot Reuse Works

The prompt cache is a **two-tier system**: an in-slot KV cache (the llama.cpp context) and an off-slot `server_prompt_cache` that stores serialized KV state on disk/memory for later restoration. The common-prefix matching is the core mechanism for deciding which cached prompt to reuse.

### 1. Slot Selection via LCP Similarity (`get_available_slot`)

When a new task arrives, `get_available_slot()` in `server-context.cpp` (line 1586) tries to find a slot whose existing prompt shares a long common prefix with the new task's tokens:

```cpp
// find the slot that has at least n% prompt similarity
if (slot_prompt_similarity != 0.0f) {
    float sim_best = 0;

    for (server_slot & slot : slots) {
        // skip if not available
        if (slot.is_processing()) continue;
        if (tokens.empty()) continue;

        // fraction of the Longest Common Prefix length with respect to the input prompt length
        const float sim_cur = float(tokens.get_common_prefix(task.tokens)) / task.tokens.size();

        // select the current slot if the criteria match
        if (sim_cur > sim_best && sim_cur > slot_prompt_similarity) {
            sim_best = sim_cur;
            ret = &slot;
        }
    }

    if (ret != nullptr) {
        const float f_keep = (sim_best*task.tokens.size()) / ret->prompt.tokens.size();
        // if we are about to lose a large portion of the existing context - save it in the prompt cache
        if (f_keep < 0.5f) {
            update_cache = true;
        }
    }
}
```

Two metrics are computed:
- **`sim_cur`** = LCP length / new task's token count — how much of the *new* prompt is covered
- **`f_keep`** = LCP length / slot's existing token count — how much of the *existing* context survives

If `f_keep < 0.5f`, the slot's current prompt is saved to the off-slot cache before being overwritten. If no slot meets the similarity threshold, an LRU fallback picks the least-recently-used idle slot.

### 2. Common-Prefix Matching (`get_common_prefix`)

The actual LCP computation is in `server-common.cpp` (line 471):

```cpp
size_t server_tokens::get_common_prefix(const server_tokens & b) const {
    const size_t max_idx = std::min(tokens.size(), b.tokens.size());

    if (!has_mtmd) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < max_idx; ++i) {
            if (tokens[i] == b.tokens[i]) {
                continue;
            }
            return i;
        }
        return max_idx;
    }

    // Multimodal path: LLAMA_TOKEN_NULL markers indicate media chunks
    for (size_t i = 0; i < max_idx; ++i) {
        const llama_token ai = tokens[i];
        const llama_token bi = b.tokens[i];

        if (ai == LLAMA_TOKEN_NULL && bi == LLAMA_TOKEN_NULL) {
            const auto & a_chunk = find_chunk(i);
            const auto & b_chunk = b.find_chunk(i);
            const std::string id_ai = mtmd_input_chunk_get_id(a_chunk.get());
            const std::string id_bi = mtmd_input_chunk_get_id(b_chunk.get());
            const size_t n_tok_a = mtmd_input_chunk_get_n_tokens(a_chunk.get());
            const size_t n_tok_b = mtmd_input_chunk_get_n_tokens(b_chunk.get());

            if (id_ai == id_bi && n_tok_a == n_tok_b) {
                i += n_tok_a - 1; // skip ahead past the media tokens
                continue;
            }
            return i;
        }

        if (ai == bi) continue;
        return i;
